WEBVTT

00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:04.107
The assistant schedules a Pix transfer.

00:00:04.407 --> 00:00:07.834
The bank takes a moment too long, and the call times out.

00:00:08.134 --> 00:00:10.403
So the code does what it always does.

00:00:10.703 --> 00:00:11.746
It tries again.

00:00:12.046 --> 00:00:13.220
Five times.

00:00:13.870 --> 00:00:16.602
But the bank had already recorded the first one.

00:00:16.902 --> 00:00:19.826
The response just got lost on the way back.

00:00:20.126 --> 00:00:22.606
Five transfers go out, for one request.

00:00:23.256 --> 00:00:26.774
In the last lesson, you saw this smell: blind retry.

00:00:27.074 --> 00:00:31.975
Now we'll fix it, step by step, with a number before and a number after.

00:00:32.275 --> 00:00:36.788
You'll leave knowing which errors deserve a second attempt, and how to make that

00:00:36.788 --> 00:00:38.142
second attempt harmless.

00:00:39.342 --> 00:00:42.195
The idea fits in one sentence.

00:00:42.495 --> 00:00:45.007
Before you retry, classify the error.

00:00:45.657 --> 00:00:48.402
A transient error can go away on its own.

00:00:48.702 --> 00:00:53.379
An unstable network, a rate limit, a service that's briefly down.

00:00:53.679 --> 00:00:57.179
Those get retries, with a growing wait and a cap.

00:00:57.829 --> 00:00:59.449
A permanent error won't.

00:00:59.749 --> 00:01:05.295
Invalid input, no permission, not enough balance, a business rule.

00:01:05.595 --> 00:01:09.713
Those go back to whoever called, with an explanation they can act on.

00:01:10.363 --> 00:01:14.703
And every call that changes the world carries an idempotency key.

00:01:15.003 --> 00:01:17.307
That's the piece that makes a retry safe.

00:01:18.507 --> 00:01:20.557
Before, there was one arrow.

00:01:20.857 --> 00:01:23.136
Any error went straight back to the call.

00:01:23.436 --> 00:01:26.327
After, the type of error picks the path.

00:01:26.977 --> 00:01:28.689
The call goes out with a key.

00:01:28.989 --> 00:01:31.964
The service uses it to recognize a repeat.

00:01:32.614 --> 00:01:36.418
The error is classified by its code or status.

00:01:36.718 --> 00:01:39.993
Never by searching the message text for the word timeout.

00:01:40.293 --> 00:01:41.545
Messages change.

00:01:41.845 --> 00:01:42.896
Codes don't.

00:01:43.546 --> 00:01:47.470
Only transient errors loop back, with a growing wait and a cap.

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:50.574
Permanent errors become a clear answer.

00:01:50.874 --> 00:01:55.667
For the user, or for the model, so it can change course instead of insisting.

00:01:56.317 --> 00:01:59.840
And success happens once, even when there were retries.

00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:05.651
Let's walk through the dangerous case, the timeout that was actually a success.

00:02:06.301 --> 00:02:10.835
A user asks to schedule two hundred and fifty reais for tomorrow.

00:02:11.485 --> 00:02:15.721
The moment they ask, the system creates a request id.

00:02:16.021 --> 00:02:18.063
Not inside the retry loop.

00:02:18.363 --> 00:02:19.105
At the click.

00:02:19.755 --> 00:02:21.692
The bank processes the transfer.

00:02:21.992 --> 00:02:24.202
Then the network drops the reply.

00:02:24.852 --> 00:02:26.652
Your code sees a timeout.

00:02:26.952 --> 00:02:29.439
That's transient, so it retries.

00:02:29.739 --> 00:02:30.804
With the same key.

00:02:31.454 --> 00:02:37.003
The bank recognizes the key, and instead of scheduling again, it returns the first

00:02:37.003 --> 00:02:37.477
result.

00:02:38.127 --> 00:02:41.050
The trace shows two attempts, and one effect.

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:43.301
That's the number you want to see.

00:02:43.951 --> 00:02:46.467
Treating the timeout as transient was right.

00:02:46.767 --> 00:02:51.938
What stopped the duplicate was a key born with the user's intent, not with the

00:02:51.938 --> 00:02:52.469
attempt.

00:02:52.769 --> 00:02:57.827
Serious payment APIs accept that key for exactly this reason.

00:02:59.027 --> 00:03:00.493
Here's one for you.

00:03:00.793 --> 00:03:04.232
An email API can send back five errors.

00:03:04.532 --> 00:03:06.105
Too many requests.

00:03:06.405 --> 00:03:08.240
Invalid recipient.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:10.180
Service unavailable.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:12.330
Invalid API key.

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:15.753
And duplicate key, message already sent.

00:03:16.053 --> 00:03:17.988
What do you do with each one?

00:03:21.038 --> 00:03:23.391
Too many requests is transient.

00:03:23.691 --> 00:03:28.153
Wait as long as the service asks, then try again, within your cap.

00:03:28.803 --> 00:03:31.136
Invalid recipient is permanent.

00:03:31.436 --> 00:03:35.589
Tell the user, or the model, that the address needs fixing.

00:03:36.239 --> 00:03:38.870
Service unavailable is transient.

00:03:39.170 --> 00:03:42.264
Retry, with a growing wait between attempts.

00:03:42.564 --> 00:03:45.793
If it keeps failing past the cap, stop and fall back.

00:03:46.443 --> 00:03:50.918
Invalid API key is permanent, and it's a configuration problem.

00:03:51.218 --> 00:03:54.155
No call will ever work, so don't retry.

00:03:54.455 --> 00:03:55.623
Alert the team.

00:03:56.273 --> 00:03:59.693
And duplicate key is a success in disguise.

00:03:59.993 --> 00:04:02.216
The email already went out.

00:04:02.516 --> 00:04:05.448
Treat it as done, and don't send it again.

00:04:06.648 --> 00:04:09.960
As with every refactoring here, start by measuring.

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:16.653
Pull from your logs the most common errors for each dependency, how many retries

00:04:16.653 --> 00:04:19.976
each one caused, and any duplicated effects.

00:04:20.276 --> 00:04:24.129
Two transfers for the same request is the number that matters.

00:04:24.779 --> 00:04:28.894
Classify each service's error codes as transient or permanent.

00:04:29.194 --> 00:04:31.355
When in doubt, treat it as permanent.

00:04:31.655 --> 00:04:35.104
A wrong no is cheaper than a wrong yes.

00:04:35.754 --> 00:04:41.627
Add an idempotency key to every call with a side effect, generated once per user

00:04:41.627 --> 00:04:42.140
intent.

00:04:42.790 --> 00:04:48.928
Then retry only transient errors, with growing waits, a cap, and a little randomness

00:04:48.928 --> 00:04:49.805
in the wait.

00:04:50.105 --> 00:04:55.047
That randomness is called jitter, and it stops a thousand clients from retrying in

00:04:55.047 --> 00:04:56.011
the same second.

00:04:56.661 --> 00:05:01.690
Write useful messages for permanent errors, for the user and for the model.

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:06.053
Not enough balance on the account is something a person can act on.

00:05:06.353 --> 00:05:07.893
Error is not.

00:05:08.543 --> 00:05:11.737
In the agent loop, add a repetition detector.

00:05:12.037 --> 00:05:16.903
The same tool, with the same arguments, after a permanent error, ends that path.

00:05:17.553 --> 00:05:18.703
And measure again.

00:05:19.003 --> 00:05:23.684
Retries per error type, time to answer, and duplicated effects.

00:05:23.984 --> 00:05:25.868
That last one should be zero.

00:05:27.068 --> 00:05:31.418
The first risk is the most common bug in this refactoring.

00:05:31.718 --> 00:05:34.774
The key is generated inside the retry loop.

00:05:35.074 --> 00:05:40.499
Every attempt gets a new key, the service sees new requests, and idempotency is

00:05:40.499 --> 00:05:40.843
gone.

00:05:41.143 --> 00:05:46.679
The key is born with the click, the ticket, the conversation, never with the

00:05:46.679 --> 00:05:47.261
attempt.

00:05:47.911 --> 00:05:49.753
The second is stacked retries.

00:05:50.053 --> 00:05:53.347
The provider's SDK retries twice.

00:05:53.647 --> 00:05:55.969
Your function retries three times.

00:05:56.269 --> 00:05:58.095
The agent calls the tool again.

00:05:58.395 --> 00:06:01.945
That's nine attempts, and nobody agreed on that number.

00:06:02.245 --> 00:06:06.609
Turn off the SDK retry on calls you classify yourself.

00:06:06.909 --> 00:06:08.670
One place decides.

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:12.056
And some services don't accept a key at all.

00:06:12.356 --> 00:06:14.972
Then check the state before repeating.

00:06:15.272 --> 00:06:18.130
Is there already a transfer for this request?

00:06:18.430 --> 00:06:21.191
If you can't check, don't retry a side effect.

00:06:21.491 --> 00:06:23.449
Hand it to a person instead.

00:06:24.649 --> 00:06:26.634
So here's the idea to take with you.

00:06:26.934 --> 00:06:30.306
A good retry policy is mostly about saying no.

00:06:30.606 --> 00:06:36.492
No to errors that time won't fix, no to new keys on old intentions, and no to a

00:06:36.492 --> 00:06:38.206
third layer of retries.

00:06:38.506 --> 00:06:41.956
What's left is a second attempt that can never do harm.

00:06:42.606 --> 00:06:48.402
In the next lesson, we'll see everything from this trail working together in a real

00:06:48.402 --> 00:06:51.195
system: invoice and contract extraction.

00:06:51.495 --> 00:06:52.324
See you there.
